WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:05.000
Speaker 1: So David Litt is a former Barack
Obama speechwriter.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:06.709
I've interviewed him on the program.

00:00:06.709 --> 00:00:07.911
Really interesting guy to talk to you.

00:00:07.911 --> 00:00:11.929
You can check out the YouTube archives or
the website archives to find my interview

00:00:11.929 --> 00:00:13.320
with David David.

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:19.710
It was invited to Newsmax yesterday, presumably
to talk about Elon Musk's hosting of Saturday

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:21.990
Night Live on Saturday evening.

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:27.740
And it went very similarly to the Fox News
clip we looked at earlier of Randi Weingarten

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:30.960
calling out Fox News while on Fox News.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:38.150
David Litt went on Newsmax and he called them
out because of their deceptive and outrageous

00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:41.260
coverage of the twenty twenty election.

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:47.280
And I have to tell you, this was done so expertly
and it starts so subtly, it's introduced so

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:52.140
smoothly that it actually takes a second for
the Newsmax host to even realize what's going

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:53.140
on.

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:57.430
David Litt says, yeah, you know, Saturday
Night Live, they wrote stuff that wasn't true

00:00:57.430 --> 00:01:01.230
and they said it to their audience in comedy
format, of course.

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:05.170
And that's what Newsmax did with the 2020
election coverage over the last several months.

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:07.400
It just wasn't comedy.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:08.400
And this is how it went.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:09.400
Speaker 2: This one.

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:10.530
David Lynch, good morning.

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:11.530
Welcome to Wake Up America.

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:12.620
Nice to see you.

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:13.830
Thanks for having me.

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:15.870
I did not watch Saturday Night Live.

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:17.670
I watched the clips, the shows on too late.

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:20.220
Quite frankly, I don't think it's that funny
anymore.

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:21.950
What do you think of Elon Musk's performance?

00:01:21.950 --> 00:01:23.450
This is the first time since twenty fifteen.

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:26.000
We've had a non athlete, non entertainer on
the show.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:29.700
The last person to do that and do well with
great ratings, our former president, Donald

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:30.700
Trump.

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:32.320
Speaker 1: Well, Rob, it's a great question.

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:35.159
I mean, what happened on SNL this weekend
was that people

00:01:35.159 --> 00:01:39.670
Speaker 6: made stuff up and then set it on
television like it's true.

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:43.000
And that actually happens pretty frequently
in American TV.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:46.909
For example, in 2020,
Speaker 1: Dominion voting system sued Newsmax

00:01:46.909 --> 00:01:49.710
over its false claims about election fraud.

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:53.470
Newsmax was lying to its own viewers and Newsmax
had to settle that lawsuit.

00:01:53.470 --> 00:01:55.300
So actually, I just need to check in.

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:58.130
Are you still telling that lie or are
Speaker 6: you telling new lies?

00:01:58.130 --> 00:01:59.580
Speaker 2: What are we talking about?

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:00.580
I'm sorry, David.

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:02.901
Are we talking about do you want to talk about
something completely unrelated and try to

00:02:02.901 --> 00:02:04.520
catch me on a Monday morning totally off topic?

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:05.690
Or do you want to talk about Elon Musk?

00:02:05.690 --> 00:02:10.170
Speaker 1: I just love the reference from
the host to Monday morning.

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:15.390
David Litt, you know, on a Tuesday afternoon,
this would be acceptable.

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:18.260
How dare you try this on me on a Monday morning?

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:21.580
And this is actually something that's done
really frequently.

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:28.030
When a host is pushed up against the wall
or made uncomfortable, they often will go

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:32.400
back to this isn't what we booked you to talk
about.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:35.540
I've sometimes done this as a guest, sometimes
guests.

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:41.050
When the guest is sandbagged, the guest will
say, you didn't book me to talk about this

00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:42.380
and it's a rhetorical crutch.

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:44.620
You just have to be prepared for how to handle
it.

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:50.560
Unless you've agreed not to talk about something,
you just have to be prepared as the host or

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:51.690
as the guest.

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:53.080
I've had situations.

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:58.500
We've had maybe two or three situations where
a guest or their publicist will say, listen,

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:05.580
please don't ask about X because there's ongoing
litigation, just can't comment on it.

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:07.500
And it's going to bring the interview to a
halt.

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:12.099
And of course, I have the option as the host
of saying, well, I will ask about it, but

00:03:12.099 --> 00:03:16.000
the guest can say I'm not able to to talk
about that right now, or it might not even

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:17.040
really be a central topic.

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:19.069
And I would say, oh, yeah, that's not the
topic of the interview.

00:03:19.069 --> 00:03:23.590
Anyway, I've been on shows where they give
me a list of eight topics and say we might

00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:25.480
talk about these things.

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:29.620
And I have said before, I don't know anything
about topic number five, so I'd rather not

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:30.620
talk about it.

00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:32.660
If you ask me, I'll just say I don't know
about that.

00:03:32.660 --> 00:03:34.260
So I'm not the right person to ask.

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:35.989
And we sort of come to an agreement.

00:03:35.989 --> 00:03:39.120
Other than that, it's basically just a rhetorical
game.

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:44.120
And sometimes when a guest will turn around
and start asking the host questions, the host

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:46.790
will do the I'm asking the questions here.

00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:48.220
You're here as my guest.

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:50.190
And then it's just sort of like a battle of
rhetoric.

00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:52.620
There's no, like, set rules about this.

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:55.030
It's really up for interpretation.

00:03:55.030 --> 00:03:58.569
And here the host really comes off like a
loser, quite frankly.

00:03:58.569 --> 00:03:59.849
Let's continue.

00:03:59.849 --> 00:04:03.210
Well, I can see why you don't want to talk
about Dominion voting systems, because if

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:07.210
you do, Newsmax could get sued and lose billions
of dollars because these are lot.

00:04:07.210 --> 00:04:08.210
David.

00:04:08.210 --> 00:04:09.210
Speaker 2: David.

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:10.210
David, David.

00:04:10.210 --> 00:04:11.459
That's lesson David.

00:04:11.459 --> 00:04:12.459
That's fine.

00:04:12.459 --> 00:04:13.620
David, I know this is a very funny moment
for you.

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:16.530
I'm sure you didn't sleep last night as you
prepared to sort of try and get the morning

00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:18.239
anchor on Newsmax.

00:04:18.239 --> 00:04:20.489
I'd be happy to talk with you about whatever
you want to talk about.

00:04:20.489 --> 00:04:23.110
Obviously, it's not the topic that we have
set up for right now.

00:04:23.110 --> 00:04:26.169
So if you'd like to talk about Saturday Night
Live, I will do that with you.

00:04:26.169 --> 00:04:30.410
But obviously, I'm not going to talk about
anything else right now.

00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:32.610
So you decide right now in this moment on
live television.

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:33.610
Go ahead.

00:04:33.610 --> 00:04:38.020
Speaker 1: The Dominion voting systems have
any impact on the 2020 election.

00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:42.360
Speaker 2: Oh, that's unfortunate because
I was really excited about this interview,

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:46.830
I didn't watch SNL on Saturday night, but
I thought Elon Musk did an OK job and he also

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:48.319
said that he's got Asperger's.

00:04:48.319 --> 00:04:51.479
So I thought that was a fascinating component
of the monologue.

00:04:51.479 --> 00:04:54.110
So, David, we look forward to having him back
on very soon again.

00:04:54.110 --> 00:04:55.330
That was a stellar interview.

00:04:55.330 --> 00:04:57.490
David, let thanks so much for joining us.

00:04:57.490 --> 00:04:58.490
We'll be right back.

00:04:58.490 --> 00:05:00.599
Speaker 1: So, yeah, just cutting off the
interview.

00:05:00.599 --> 00:05:07.599
And it reminds me, by the way, the mention
of Asperger's or as the host says, Asperger's,

00:05:07.599 --> 00:05:12.550
there's a reason we no longer say Asperger's
and instead say, you know, high functioning

00:05:12.550 --> 00:05:16.659
autism or low, low severity autism spectrum
disorder.

00:05:16.659 --> 00:05:23.370
Dr. Hans Asperger did work linked to Naziism,
Nazi child psychiatry.

00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:28.669
There's a debate because Asperger himself,
I guess, was a resister, a Nazi resister to

00:05:28.669 --> 00:05:29.719
the Nazis.

00:05:29.719 --> 00:05:34.409
But many children that were identified by
him as having so-called Asperger's were subsequently

00:05:34.409 --> 00:05:35.800
killed by Nazis.

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:41.369
So I was actually surprised that Elon Musk
even used that term to describe his own condition.

00:05:41.369 --> 00:05:43.069
Maybe that's a topic for another day.

00:05:43.069 --> 00:05:46.240
I like what David did here.

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:50.770
It did get cut off pretty quickly, but there's
really no other way to pierce the Newsmax

00:05:50.770 --> 00:05:56.699
